
--> Secured Swagger APIs
    Why Swagger Needs Security
    Most real-world APIs are secured using:
    JWT tokens
    OAuth2
    Basic authentication
    Swagger must support these security mechanisms so developers can:
    Test secured APIs
    Validate authentication flows
    Avoid disabling security for testing


    How Swagger Handles Security (Conceptually)
    Swagger allows defining security schemes, such as:
    Bearer Token (JWT)
    API Keys
    OAuth2 flows
    Once configured:
    Swagger UI provides an Authorize button
    Users can enter credentials or tokens
    Swagger automatically sends auth data with requests


    Why Secured Swagger Is Important
    Prevents exposing sensitive APIs
    Allows safe testing of protected endpoints
    Maintains production-level security
    Avoids security bypass during development
    In production systems:
    Swagger access itself is often restricted to non-public environments

